    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Abdulaziz recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1902, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Ahsa</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1913 with the Ikhwan, conquered the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> in 1924–25 and, after crushing the Ikhwan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sabilla">Battle of Sabilla</a> in 1929, united Hejaz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> as Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> on 23 September 1932.</li><li>Oil found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Ahsa</a> in 1938 and developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco">Aramco</a> from 1941 made the kingdom the world's largest oil producer by 1976; the 1973 embargo quadrupled oil prices, and the state bought out Aramco's American owners in 1980.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> rule as absolute monarchs under the Basic Law of 1992, with uncodified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> and the ulema, led by the Al ash-Sheikh, sharing power in a pact dating from 1744; no political parties or national elections are permitted.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>'s slave population was estimated at 300,000 as recently as the early 1960s; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was officially abolished only in 1962.</p></aside>
    </section>

