    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-saud.jpg" alt="Ibn Saud" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Portrait_of_King_Abdulaziz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudi Press Agency, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> emerged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, where Muhammad bin Saud became emir on 22 February 1727. In 1744 he joined forces with the religious leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a>, founder of the strict Wahhabi movement within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, an alliance that drove Saudi expansion and still underpins the dynasty's rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Diriyah sacked Karbala in 1802 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1803 before the Ottoman viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Mohammed Ali Pasha, destroyed it in 1818; the smaller Emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> of 1824 lost the interior to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidi-dynasty">Al Rashid</a> of Jabal Shammar, and by 1891 the Al Saud were exiles in Kuwait.</p><p>In 1902 Abdul Rahman's son Abdulaziz, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> and founded the third Saudi state. With the Ikhwan, a tribal army inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahhabism">Wahhabism</a> and led by Faisal Al-Dawish, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Ahsa</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1913, stayed out of the Arab Revolt of 1916 that made Hussein bin Ali King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, defeated the Al Rashid and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Nejd in 1921. The Kingdom of Hejaz fell in 1924–25 and on 10 January 1926 he declared himself its king, ruling the two realms separately. When the Ikhwan raided British Transjordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Kuwait and turned against his modernising policies, he crushed them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sabilla">Battle of Sabilla</a> in 1929, where their leaders were massacred. On 23 September 1932 Prince Faisal declared the unification of Hejaz and Nejd as the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco.jpg" alt="Saudi Aramco" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco">Saudi Aramco</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riyadh_Metro_(2024).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudi Press Agency (SPA), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new kingdom lived on limited agriculture and pilgrimage revenues until vast oil reserves were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Ahsa</a> in 1938. Full-scale development began in 1941 under the American-controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco">Aramco</a>, and oil brought prosperity and political leverage, though wasteful spending left large deficits and foreign borrowing by the 1950s. The state took 20% of Aramco in 1972 and bought out the American interests in 1980; by 1976 it was the world's largest oil producer. Oil still supplies about 63% of budget revenue, 67% of export earnings and 45% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, and the kingdom holds the second-largest proven reserves of petroleum. Its economy, the largest in the Arab world and 18th in the world, exceeds $1.1 trillion nominally.</p><p>Oil wealth transformed a society that in 1950 numbered some 3 million, mostly nomadic pastoralists and farmers. The population reached 32 million in 2022, about 85% of them urban in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dammam">Dammam</a>, half under 25 and close to 42% immigrants; about 80% of private-sector employees are foreign, many under the kafala system that rights groups link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Slavery itself, with an estimated 300,000 enslaved people in the early 1960s, was abolished in 1962. The 1970s brought large-scale wheat farming on non-renewable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>, four-fifths of which was gone by 2012; desalination now gives half the drinking water. The kingdom is the world's second producer of dates and hosts some two million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> pilgrims a year.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> was made by conquest and has spent heavily on arms ever since. Its Armed Forces under the Ministry of Defence comprise the Royal Saudi Land Forces with the Royal Guard, the Air Force, the Navy, the Air Defence and the Strategic Missile Force; the Saudi Arabian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>, under its own ministry, is drawn from tribal forces loyal to the royal family. In 2023 there were 127,000 active personnel in the Armed Forces, 130,000 in the National Guard and 24,500 in the paramilitary forces. Spending of about $80.3 billion in 2024, roughly 7% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, ranked seventh in the world; the kingdom was the second-largest arms importer of 2019–2023 and the largest recipient of American arms.</p><p>The Ikhwan wars of the 1920s ended at Sabilla in 1929. Saudi Arabia and Kuwait spent $25 billion backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a> of 1980–1988, yet after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s invasion of Kuwait in 1990 King Fahd invited American and coalition troops onto Saudi soil, and in 1991 Saudi forces flew bombing raids and joined the land invasion that liberated Kuwait. In March 2015 the kingdom mobilised 150,000 troops and 100 fighter jets against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> by early 2016 while Houthi attacks crossed the border; 2,500 Saudi troops were still engaged in 2024. A mutual defence pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> followed in September 2025, and the kingdom has said it would seek nuclear weapons if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> acquires them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. The Basic Law adopted by royal decree in 1992 binds the king to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> and declares the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the Sunnah the constitution; the king combines legislative, executive and judicial power, royal decrees form the legislation, and no political parties or national elections exist. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a>, at least 7,000 princes with power concentrated in some 200 male descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, hold the key ministries and the thirteen regional governorships. Succession passed from Ibn Saud to Saud (1953), Faisal (1964), Khalid (1975), Fahd (1982) and Abdullah (2005), and the Allegiance Council of 2007 regulates it.</p><p>The ulema have a direct role in government unique among modern states, led by the Al ash-Sheikh, descendants of Ibn Abd al-Wahhab, in a mutual support pact with the Al Saud nearly 300 years old; after the Grand Mosque seizure of 1979 their control of education and social behaviour grew, and King Abdullah later trimmed it. Sharia is uncodified, judges follow the Hanbali school, final appeal lies with the king, and retaliatory punishments and diyya remain, though flogging was abolished in 2020 and codification was announced in 2021. Participation is limited to the majlis, where men may petition the king, the Consultative Council of 1993 whose sixty members the king chooses, and municipal elections since 2005. Freedom House rates the kingdom Not Free, 8 of 100 in 2023, and open protest is not tolerated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mecca.jpg" alt="Mecca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_Mecca1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudipics.com, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As custodian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> the kingdom stands at the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and its oil at the centre of the world economy. It co-founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a> in 1960 and has officially sought to stabilise the oil market, yet the 1973 embargo on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and other supporters of Israel caused an oil crisis with lasting effects on global politics. Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> has been a strategic ally of the United States since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and its leading arms customer, signing letters of intent for $350 billion of purchases in 2017. It is a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-cooperation-council">Gulf Cooperation Council</a>, the Muslim World League and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation and the only Arab state in the G20.</p><p>Since 1979 the kingdom has fought a proxy rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and used its wealth to contain revolutionary change in its neighbours, from the Arab Spring to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. The export of Wahhabi doctrine and private Saudi funding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> militant groups drew accusations of sponsoring terrorism, sharpened when fifteen of the September 11 hijackers proved to be Saudi nationals and answered by the Grand Mufti's 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> against ISIS and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> and the Islamic Military Counter Terrorism Coalition of 2015; since 2016 the state has stopped funding mosques abroad. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-salman">Mohammed bin Salman</a> it has leaned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, coordinated oil cuts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and sought defence ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-salman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-salman.jpg" alt="Mohammed bin Salman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-salman">Mohammed bin Salman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%B3%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D9%84%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%85%D9%8A%D8%B1_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B3%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B2%D9%8A%D8%B2_%D8%A2%D9%84_%D8%B3%D8%B9%D9%88%D8%AF_(%D9%85%D9%82%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B5%D8%A9).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">وكالة الأنباء السعودية, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the twenty-first century the kingdom was extremely wealthy yet near stagnant, with unemployment and discontent at the royal family's monopoly of power. The Arab Spring reached Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in 2011; King Abdullah answered with $36 billion and then $93 billion of benefits and housing, and no political reform, though women voted in the municipal elections of 2015. Since 2017 Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-salman">Mohammed bin Salman</a> has ruled in practice: as many as 500 princes, ministers and businessmen were arrested in his anti-corruption campaign of November 2017, women were allowed to drive in June 2018, and the religious police were curbed, while mass executions in 2016, 2019, 2022 and 2025 and the detention of women in the Dar al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reaya">Reaya</a> care homes drew condemnation.</p><p>The economy is being remade under Vision 2030: services were privatised from 2003, economic cities planned, a tourist visa for non-Muslims opened in 2019 and, in 2024, joint ventures formed with Chinese firms to localise 75% of renewable components by 2030. The pandemic and falling oil prices brought what the finance minister in May 2020 called the first severe crisis in decades. Water scarcity has broken much of the kingdom's agriculture and sent it to buy farmland abroad. With 2,500 troops still in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 2024, a defence pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 2025 and relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> cooling through dialogue, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a> governs a young, urban population of 32 million as it has since 1932.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> was unified on 23 September 1932 by Abdulaziz <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, heir to the Wahhabi alliance of 1744, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and eastern Arabia. Oil found in 1938 made it the world's leading exporter and a strategic ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> still rule as absolute monarchs under the Basic Law of 1992, guardians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and the largest economy of the Arab world.</em></p>

